  "independent_source_observations": [
    "The opening contains two separately addressed letters: 1:1 addresses Jews in Egypt, and 1:10 addresses Aristobulus and Egyptian Jews. The Chislev observance is expressed through Booths and purification language; identifying it with the ordinary seventh-month Feast of Booths would be inaccurate.",
    "The Nanea account in 1:13-16 has a ruler named Antiochus trapped, stoned, dismembered, and beheaded in the temple; chapter 9 has Antiochus retreat after a failed robbery at Persepolis, suffer illness and a chariot fall, write a letter, and die in the mountains. A relationship must preserve these differences and must not present one continuous death scene.",
    "Nehemiah altar-fire tradition in 1:18-36 and Jeremiah concealment tradition in 2:1-8 are narrative claims in this text. Shared names or objects do not establish that other texts narrate these same particular events.",
    "2:4 locates Jeremiah on the mountain where Moses viewed the inheritance; 2:8-12 explicitly invokes Moses and Solomon, glory/cloud, heavenly fire, the sin offering, and an eight-day dedication. The exact source verses and Greek numbering require inspection.",
    "Onias the high priest, Jason his brother, and Menelaus the brother of Simon are distinguished in 3:1, 4:7, and 4:23. Andronicus who murders Onias and is executed in 4:34-38 must not be conflated with the Gerizim overseer named Andronicus in 5:23. Apollonius patronymics/roles also differ across 3:5, 4:4/21, and 5:24.",
    "5:18 explicitly recalls Heliodorus and 5:27 underlies the wilderness recollection in 10:6. 6:12-16 and 7:18/32-38 distinguish disciplinary suffering of the people from the tyrant's eventual judgment.",
    "The martyr speeches in 7:9/11/14/22-23/28-29 affirm creation and restoration of life; a cross-reference needs an actual shared claim rather than a general topic of death or courage.",
    "8:19 expressly cites Sennacherib and 185000 deaths. The Galatian battle of 8:20 is a distinct recollection. The Nicanor campaign in 8:9-36 must not be merged with later Nicanor encounters or with every campaign of Timothy/Bacchides.",
    "8:22 calls the division leaders Simon, Joseph, and Jonathan; source wording must govern any comparison. Eleazar the martyr in chapter 6 cannot be assumed identical to Eleazar in 8:23."
  ],
